From u63023c771a070260 at groups.google.com  Sat Feb 14 01:23:04 2015
From: u63023c771a070260 at groups.google.com (Antonio Piccolboni)
Date: Sat, 14 Feb 2015 01:23:04 +0000
Subject: rmr2 3.3.1 and plyrmr 0.6.0 released
Message-ID: <qxuue5utYg8.6K2e8l3srUYJ@rhadoop.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rhadoop/c/qxuue5utYg8/m/6K2e8l3srUYJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,
rmr2 3.3.1 is a bugfix release. plyrmr 0.6.0 introduces support for a spark backend. It was there in 0.5.0, still incomplete. Now it passes all the tests as the Mapreduce backend. I am sure there still more work ahead and that the api is not settled, hence this release is still 0.x series and marked as a pre-release, but it's already usable and it's a great time to provide feedback.

Links:

https://github.com/RevolutionAnalytics/plyrmr/releases
https://github.com/RevolutionAnalytics/rmr2/releases

As you might have noticed, we switched to using the releases feature of github. The "new in this release" and "changelog" pages are replaced by the releases page. The downloads page is still there but it's no longer necessary for rmr2 and plyrmr, just go to the releases page directly. Below the text, are the links to the actual builds (win build included for rmr2). Then there are links to source code archives, which for these projects include the R pacakge source but also additional documentation and older builds (which are progressively removed). Not sure these latter links are so useful, if you want to participate to the project cloning is the way. The additional documents can be accessed directly in the browser.

 Thanks

Antonio

From u5157fefdb1b66e1c at groups.google.com  Wed Feb 18 11:53:47 2015
From: u5157fefdb1b66e1c at groups.google.com (amitai golub)
Date: Wed, 18 Feb 2015 11:53:47 +0000
Subject: plyrmr::dcast memory issues
Message-ID: <irqVT9DdYVs.iqF6FMkBFCoJ@rhadoop.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rhadoop/c/irqVT9DdYVs/m/iqF6FMkBFCoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I've been working with the RHadoop packages for about two months now, and have been running into some problems here and there that often relate to space, which I feel that my cluster should be able to handle but for some reason doesn't.

In particular, I have a melted table (in hdfs, in a hive-ish format) that has 5 columns, like such:

> head(my.tbl)

          loc   id          date     pivot    cat
1          aa    1    2013-04-07       d60      1
2          aa    1    2013-04-07       d90      0
3          aa    2    2013-04-07       d60      0
4          aa    2    2013-04-07       d90      0
5          aa    3    2013-04-07       d60      3

The full table has ~100K rows. I'm trying to run the following commad:

> dcast(my.tbl, formula = loc + id + date ~ pivot, value.var = 'cat')

for 100 lines, this works fine. For 10K lines this breaks down (the map job runs for a while and then hangs at some percentage). Increasing the memory via 'plyrmr.options' does help, by getting the 10K job through, but not enough to solve the table as a whole. How does this scale?

I've looked at the source code for "dcast.pipe" and have tried to run it line by line, and the function seemed to seize up at the grouping stage. Could it be connected to the format (when calling the data, R reads the data correctly)?

Thanks for the help!!!
Amitai

